## 💡 Key Findings & Insights

- The dataset contains 1,599 red wine samples and 12 columns.
- Medium-quality wine represents approximately 82.45% of the dataset after categorization.
- Low-quality wine represents approximately 3.94% of the dataset.
- High-quality wine represents approximately 13.57% of the dataset.
- Alcohol has the strongest positive correlation with the original quality score (0.4762).
- Volatile acidity has the strongest negative correlation with quality (-0.3906).
- Volatile acidity, sulphates, and alcohol are the three most important features according to the Random Forest model.
- Random Forest achieved the highest accuracy and macro F1 Score among the three tested models.

## 🏆 Conclusion

The project successfully demonstrates how Machine Learning classification algorithms can be used to predict wine quality categories based on physicochemical properties.

Among the three implemented models, Random Forest performed best, achieving 83.75% accuracy and a macro F1 Score of 55.06%.

However, the dataset has an imbalanced target distribution, and the lower performance on the Low-quality category indicates that minority-class prediction remains a challenge.

